\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Unless a section says otherwise, ${\mathbb{N}}=\{1,2,\ldots\}$,
${\mathbb{Z}}$ is the ring of integers, ${\mathbb{Q}},{\mathbb{R}},{\mathbb{C}}$ are the rational, real, and complex
fields, $[N]=\{1,\ldots,\lfloor N\rfloor\}$, and $\log$ is the natural
logarithm. For real functions of a parameter tending to infinity,
$f\ll g$ and $f=O(g)$ mean $|f|\leq Cg$ eventually for some fixed $C$;
$f\gg g$ reverses this comparison; $f=o(g)$ means $f/g\to0$;
$f\sim g$ means $f/g\to1$; and $f\asymp g$ means both order bounds.
A subscript on $O$ or $\ll$ specifies allowed constant dependence.
The expression $n^{o(1)}$ in an upper bound means growth smaller than
$n^\epsilon$ for each fixed $\epsilon>0$. Local definitions govern any
exception, finite-field convention, or use of the same symbol for another
object. An asymptotic claim is not automatically an effective algorithm.



For $0<\varepsilon<1/2$, let $P_\varepsilon=\{(x,y):\min_{m\in{\mathbb{Z}}}|x-m|\leq\varepsilon\}$. A permitted motion is a rotation $R_\theta$ about the origin, with no translation. Let $m(\varepsilon)$ be the minimum size of a finite family of angles for which $\bigcup_j R_{\theta_j}P_\varepsilon={\mathbb{R}}^2$, taking $m=\infty$ if none exists. Determine this number or its sharp growth as $\varepsilon\downarrow0$. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]} \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1305.1514}{[S2]}

\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
How many rotated (about the origin) copies of the "pyjama set" $\{(x, y) \in \mathbb{R}^2 : \operatorname{dist}(x, \mathbb{Z}) \leq \varepsilon\}$ are needed to cover $\mathbb{R}^2$? \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}

\paragraph{Residual task reported by the archive.}

The embedded review (2026-08-17) records: Obtain substantially smaller upper bounds, such as epsilon\textasciicircum{}{-C}, and nontrivial matching lower bounds for the minimum covering number. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
How many differently rotated families of narrow periodic strips are needed to cover the whole plane?

